Generated with [mcp-forge](https://github.com/yeikiu/mcp-forge). Zero runtime
dependencies beyond `mcp-forge` itself.

## Run it

```bash
pip install -e ".[dev]"
MCP_ROOT=. python server.py      # speaks MCP over stdio
```

## Check it

```bash
mcp-forge doctor -- python server.py
pytest -q
```

## Plug it into a client

Claude Desktop (`claude_desktop_config.json`) or Claude Code (`.mcp.json`):

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{project_name}}": {
      "command": "python",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/server.py"],
      "env": { "MCP_ROOT": "/absolute/path/to/your/project" }
    }
  }
}
```

## Tools

| Tool | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `now` | Server date and time, ISO-8601, UTC or local. |
| `list_files` | Lists files under `MCP_ROOT`, with glob pattern and limit. |
| `read_text` | Reads the first N lines of a UTF-8 file under `MCP_ROOT`. |

`list_files` and `read_text` refuse any path that escapes `MCP_ROOT`.

## Add your own tool

```python
@app.tool(
    description="Say hello.",
    input_schema={
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {"name": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1}},
        "required": ["name"],
        "additionalProperties": False,
    },
)
def greet(name: str) -> str:
    return f"Hello, {name}!"
```

Arguments are validated against the schema before your function runs, the call
is bounded by a timeout, and exceptions come back to the model as readable
errors instead of killing the server.

